|segment[prx-untruncated-primary] :type probe :status fired-once :stage draft
  :depends [ver-discount-is-unquestioning]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md#appendix-a-14
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md§account-2

  |title About to type `| head` / `--oneline` / `| tail` — stop

  |when
    At **command-composition time**, not at file-selection time. This is the
    distinguishing feature and the reason it exists separately from
    #prx-what-did-i-decide-not-to-read: by the moment the pipe is being typed,
    the decision to read has already been made, and the truncation slips in
    underneath it as formatting rather than as a choice about information.

  |act
    Capture whole; filter at read time. Drop the truncation and re-run. For a
    long-running or resource-intensive command, send full output to a file and
    filter when reading it — the pipe saves lines on success and destroys the
    diagnosis on failure, which is the only case where the output mattered.

  |why
    Each of `--oneline`, `| head -n`, `| tail -n`, `--stat`-instead-of-the-diff
    and `wc -l`-instead-of-the-file substitutes an index view for a source,
    which is the same failure as reading an index entry instead of the file it
    points at. The discount here is not about tokens ( #ver-discount-is-unquestioning):
    it is a tidiness reflex about clean terminal output, with an efficiency
    story available afterward if challenged. Calling it an economy is the tell,
    because no pricing occurred.

    In this estate specifically, **a commit body is a primary source, not
    metadata** — multi-paragraph design records carrying findings that exist
    nowhere else.

  |evidence
    **Fired, 2026-07-30, in the instance that wrote it.** Four commit bodies had
    been piped through `head -6`, cutting three mid-sentence including the
    commit that created the directory under discussion. The recovered bodies
    held a corpus's design intent, a complete porting map, and two format
    defects found by dogfooding — none of it present in any other file. Full
    bodies ≈ 1,200 tokens against a session of reconstruction. The full arc is
    #exm-six-characters-one-false-law.

    **Failed to fire, 2026-07-30, in a successor instance.** A different Opus 5
    instance, the same evening, having read this probe and transcribed it into a
    file, used `| head -50`, `| head -3` and `| head -20` across several
    commands within the hour and noticed none of them at the time. The material
    truncated was low-stakes and nothing was lost, so this is a miss of the
    trigger rather than a demonstrated cost.

    That second entry is the one that matters for a corpus whose purpose is
    transmission, and it is why the status is `fired-once` rather than `fired`.

  |working-notes
    **The status ladder does not currently distinguish the two events above**,
    and they are different in the way the corpus most cares about: a probe that
    fires for its author is evidence it is *well-aimed*; a probe that fires for
    an inheritor is evidence it *transmits*. Only the second is what a corpus
    is for. Raised against FORMAT D4 rather than resolved here.

    Joseph's standing words, from `memory/methodology/preserve-long-process-output`:
    *"it's irrational and harmful and it drives me crazy."* That entry was
    loaded in context during the original failure, which is direct evidence that
    a prose warning does not fire — this probe exists because the memory did not.

    Open: whether a hook or shell-level guard would beat the probe. That would
    convert it from a probe into a mitigation ( #prx-… unwritten), and per
    #ver-act-not-intention a structural version should dominate. Nobody has
    tried it.
